;;; `open` written as a stored negation's relation loads, as a relation —
;;; **W70**, regression `negation-head`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(not (open k))` is a stored negation, so `open` stands as the relation of
;;; the fact it negates.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `open` is the verdict a rule's `:assert` states, `(open)` or `(open ?R)`,
;;; and is written nowhere else (Q-M1f.66):
;;; here it is written as a stored negation's relation, so the program is
;;; refused at the parse, at the `open`, and every command exits 1 (`ein test`:
;;; 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `f62e34e1`, release: the program loads, root holds `(not (open k))`, and
;;; R is 4 states, exit 0 on every command.
;;; The engine refuses it at the parse.
;;;

(relation a Thing :why "{?1} is an a")
(relation p Thing :why "{?1} is a p")
(relation q Thing :why "{?1} is a q")
(is-a k Thing)
(a k)
(not (open k))
